From 09b3ea8c130d459096e846c96d12d3c2522a5db1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 01:43:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the Matroska editions of a file over HTTP, read
 by byte ranges

The previous commit read the editions only of files on this device or
the local network, so the question was never asked for what is played
most: the files of add-ons over HTTP. They are read again, but only what
names the editions, and still without blocking the interface.

- A file over HTTP (http, https, dav, davs; for an add-on's item the URL
  it resolved to, CApplicationPlay::ResolvePath(), never a plugin://
  path) is read by byte ranges (MatroskaHttpRange.h). First a HEAD with
  "Range: bytes=0-65535": the server accepts ranges if it answers 206
  with a Content-Range that gives the length of the file, or 200 with
  "Accept-Ranges: bytes" and a Content-Length; otherwise nothing of the
  file is downloaded, and kodi.log says so. Then GETs of bounded ranges,
  each a 206 of exactly the range asked, without a content coding:
  anything else is refused at its first byte, and a body longer than
  asked where it passes that length. An HTTP error (429, 403...) or a
  network error ends the reading and the file plays as it is stored:
  nothing is ever asked again.
- What is read (MatroskaHeaderRead.h): the EBML header and the start of
  the Segment, 64 KiB; then only the elements that the SeekHead points
  to and that are needed: the Chapters and the Info where they are not
  in that range, a SeekHead it points to while one of those is missing,
  and Tags only where an edition is named by a tag (16 KiB a request
  where the size of the element is not known yet). 1 MiB at most in all:
  Chapters that do not fit are not read. Element for element as
  matroskadec.c with ffmpeg-9940 reads them, so that MKV_EDITIONS::Parse()
  gives the File that the player's demuxer gives.
- No connection more. CCurlFile's sessions are kept by protocol and
  server (DllLibCurlGlobal::easy_acquire()), and curl keeps the
  connections in the multi handle of the session. The reading takes the
  session as CCurlFile::Open() takes it, with Kodi's own options
  (SetCommonOptions()), and releases it: the player's own opening of the
  file takes the same session and finds the connection open, if it comes
  within the 30 s after which an idle session is closed (CheckIdle()) and
  before the server closes it. The transfers of the reading end as whole
  responses, which leave the connection open.
- The busy dialog, its cancel and the 10 s deadline stay; over HTTP the
  reading itself stops within 100 ms of either. What was read of a path
  is kept for the session, the last 32 (a resume does not read it
  again); a reading the user cancelled is not kept.
- A file without editions that FFmpeg's demuxer opens is now read, with
  none (it was "could not read").
- Kodi closing: CApplication::Stop() tells every reading left running to
  stop and waits for it (MKV_EDITIONS::StopReadings()), before the
  network services and the SMB client go away: over HTTP within 100 ms
  (2 s at most), on SMB or NFS until its call to the server returns, at
  the timeout of that client (30 s by default), so that it never uses
  the SMB client that is freed right after. One still running after that
  is said in the log.

Measured on the host (http_read_test: the reader and the transport as
Kodi builds them, against a local server in a network namespace of the
test, with an easy and a multi handle of libcurl kept as Kodi keeps its
sessions): a file with the Chapters at the start, 43 MB, 2 requests and
65536 bytes; with the Chapters at the end, after a 5 MB attachment and
40 MB, 3 requests and 66148 bytes; the reading and the player's opening
on one connection; a server without ranges, the HEAD only and no byte of
the file; 429 and 403, one request each and never again; a server that
does not answer, ended at the deadline, or 300 ms after the stop. The
keys and values exported are those of mkvdump with ffmpeg-9940 on the
same files, also where matroskadec.c has its own ways (a TimestampScale
of 0, the last of two EditionStrings, an integer without bytes, a NUL in
a title, the TagTargets of a Tag, a nested ChapterAtom, two Chapters
elements); 16 mutations make the test fail. Not run in Kodi on a device.
